## What the panels weigh

### Ballast, not bolts

A system adds **12 to 20 kg per m²**, about the weight of _five centimetres of wet snow_. Its output is given in kWp, and a roof's area in m<sup>2</sup>; the panels save about 0.2 t of CO<sub>2</sub> per kWp a year.[^1] Read [how we survey a roof](/insights/can-your-roof-carry-solar-panels/).

#### What the survey checks

- The load the roof can still carry
  - From the drawings, or measured
- The membrane's age

1. Read the drawings
2. Walk the roof

##### A note on old roofs

###### Before 1980

Roofs this old were often built for less snow than the standards ask for today.

> The roof decides how large the system can be, long before the budget does.
>
> <cite>Priya Raman, Head of Engineering and Delivery</cite>

| Mounting | Added load | Holes |
| --- | ---: | --- |
| Ballasted, east–west | 12–20 kg per m² | None |
| Fixed to the deck | 9–13 kg per m² | Several hundred |

